---
/**
 * Foreign stocks: why hold them, how much, how to get there if you are adding money, and
 * three sentences on currency. The worst run behind the US carries a figure record.
 */
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="International"
  description="Foreign stocks spread ownership across markets. Compare valuation, currency exposure and the cost of changing your current allocation."
>
  <Hero
    title="Should you hold foreign stocks?"
    lede="Owning foreign stocks reduces reliance on one country. It also brings different currencies, valuations and long periods behind the US. It can diversify your portfolio without protecting you from every stock-market crash."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="Probably" gloss="A global allocation is a reasonable starting point; the best regional split remains uncertain." />
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <h2 id="not-protection">Diversification is not crash insurance</h2>
    <p>Foreign and US stocks can fall together in a global crisis, and currency moves can make a foreign
    loss larger for a US investor. That does not remove the benefit of owning businesses in different markets.
    A correlation between regional investment styles cannot settle the value of geographic diversification.</p>
    <p>Valuation is another reason to compare regions. US stocks cost about 41 years of average profits. On a
    count that puts every country on the same footing, at the end of June 2026, they cost 1.7 times what the
    rest of the developed world costs and 1.85 times what emerging markets cost. Of the 4.7% a year by which US
    stocks beat foreign stocks over the 35 years to 2024, about 3.8% came from US stocks becoming more expensive
    and 1.1% from faster earnings growth. Japan, Korea
    and Taiwan are priced at or above the US, so "international is cheap" is true of Europe and emerging markets
    rather than of everywhere.</p>
    <p>Across 18 countries over 1870 to 2020, a market this expensive relative to others tended to earn about
    1.4% a year less over the next ten years, inside a range running between −7% and +4%, and the "US is
    expensive" call has been wrong every year since 2011. Treat it as a reason to hold some of your stocks
    outside the most expensive market in the world, and never as a forecast.</p>

    <h2 id="how-much">How much, and how to get there</h2>
    <p>The world's stock market is about 64% US by size, so the 65/35 US/foreign split every portfolio here uses
    is roughly the world market itself. A 60/40 split holds 4% more in foreign stocks than the world does and
    50/50 holds 14% more; 60/40 is the first stop, and I stop there because I cannot say more.</p>
    <p>If you want to change the split, new contributions and distributions can reduce the need to sell.
    How long that takes depends on the size of the change and how much you add. If cash flows are too small,
    compare a taxable sale with the risk of keeping the current allocation. Your gains, tax rate and reasons
    for changing matter; there is no universal rule to sell or stay put.</p>

    <h2 id="currency">Currency</h2>
    <p>Holding a foreign stock means holding its currency too, and whether that adds or subtracts return on
    average cannot be shown either way. Hedging the currency removes 17% to 21% of a foreign holding's swings,
    and the research's answer is to hedge about half of the developed-market holding in a retirement account
    only, because a hedged fund pays out large taxable gains in a strong-dollar year. No fund hedges what these
    portfolios own, so the funds here are unhedged.</p>

    <h2 id="what-losing-looks-like">What losing looks like</h2>
    <p>Foreign stocks trailed US stocks for 18 years, between February 2008 and November 2024, and finished
    <Figure id="intl-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} />, without catching up. That happened once already, in
    living memory, and it can happen again. Hold foreign stocks because you have decided the price is right and
    you cannot pick the winner, and not because you expect it to feel good.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>Keep the 65/35 split the portfolios use. If you are adding money and want to lean further, send new
      contributions toward the regional split you choose. Review taxes and trading costs before selling
      existing holdings, using the routine on <a href="/how-to-hold/#rebalancing">how to hold it</a>.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/portfolios/value-lean/">The value-lean portfolio</Button>
    <Button href="/strategies/" variant="outline">Every other idea</Button>
  </div>
</Base>
